.TH MTAB 5
.SH NAME
mtab \- mounted file system table
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mtab
resides in directory
.I /etc
and contains a table of devices mounted by the
.I mount
command.
.I Umount
removes entries.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.B "the table is a text file here, not v7's binary record."
Each entry is one line, and the fields are separated by blanks:
the name of the block special file, as
.I mount
was given it;
the directory it was mounted on;
and, when the file system was mounted read-only,
the word
.BR ro .
Trailing slashes are stripped from both names and nothing else is;
in particular the special file keeps its directories,
where v7 threw away everything through the last `/'.
.PP
.nf
/dev/md1 /mnt
/dev/md2 /usr/src ro
.fi
.PP
v7's entry is 64 bytes \- two null-padded 32-byte name fields, written by
copying the program's own
.B "struct mtab"
array straight to the file.
That arithmetic does not survive this machine.
A
.B char[32]
occupies 32/6 rounded up = 6 words = 36 char-units and the next member of a
structure starts on a word boundary, so
.B "sizeof(struct mtab)"
is 72 rather than 64:
v7's own code writes 64-byte records out of 72-byte objects,
every entry after the first is read back eight bytes out of step,
and
.I mount
with no argument prints rubbish.
See
.IR cmd/mount/mtab.c ,
which is the one implementation of this format and is shared by both programs.
.PP
The number of entries is bounded by
.B NMOUNT
in
.RI < sys/param.h >,
which is the number of file systems the kernel can have mounted at once.
It is 8 on this system and one of the eight is the root,
which never appears here,
so this file holds at most seven.
.PP
No file is shipped on the root image.
A missing
.I /etc/mtab
reads as an empty table, and the first
.I mount
of a boot creates it.
.PP
This table is present only so people can look at it.
It does not matter to
.I mount
if there are duplicated entries nor
to
.I umount
if a name cannot be found \- though this
.I umount
does say so, on the standard error, having just unmounted the file system anyway.
.SH FILES
/etc/mtab
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mount(1M)
